ed from\nL373: \nL374:     ==========\nL375:     Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #1 id:1 of\nL376:     cite21†https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002/ )\nL377: \nL378:     Reason for revert:\nL379:     ANGLE compilation issues should be fixed by\nL380:     cite22†https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/557679†chromium-review.googlesource.com L440:     ==========\nL441:     Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #1 id:1 of\nL442:     cite21†https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002/ )\nL443: \nL444:     Reason for revert:\nL445:     ANGLE compilation issues should be fixed by\nL446:     cite22†https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/557679†chromium-review.googlesource.com L447: \nL448:     Original issue's description:\nL449:     > Revert of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #5 id:180001 of\nL450:     cite23†https://codereview.chromium.org/2933573002/ )\nL451:     >\nL452:     > Reason for revert:\nL536: commit-bot: I haz the power\nL537: Description was changed from ========== Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #1 ...\nL538: 3 years, 5 months ago (2017-06-30 06:46:37 UTC) cite76†#13 L539: Message was sent while issue was closed.\nL540: \nL541:     Description was changed from\nL542: \nL543:     ==========\nL544:     Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #1 id:1 of\nL545:     cite21†https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002/ )\nL546: \nL547:     Reason for revert:\nL548:     ANGLE compilation issues should be fixed by\nL610:     ==========\nL611:     Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #1 id:1 of\nL612:     cite21†https://codereview.chromium.org/2965693002/ )\nL613: \nL614:     Reason for revert:\nL615:     ANGLE compilation issues should be fixed by\nL616:     cite22†https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/557679†chromium-review.googlesource.com L617: \nL618:     Original issue's description:\nL619:     > Revert of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ (patchset #5 id:180001 of\nL620:     cite23†https://codereview.chromium.org/2933573002/ )\nL621:     >\nL622:     > Reason for revert:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nabsl::Mutex in Chromium? (https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/cxx/c/bIlGr1URn8I)\nciteturn32view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn25view1\",\"pattern\":\"turned off\"}); Total lines: 1110\nL1037: There's a bunch of details/investigation on the bug (cite39†https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=1123627†bugs.chromium.org ), but basically our understanding was that this only helped on Windows because of the fact that Windows' underlying locks can suffer from priority inversion, whereas other platforms have kernel support for detecting and preventing priority inversion (i.e. boosting the low-priority thread if a high-priority thread is waiting for the lock).\nL1038: So there, we were just experiencing the overhead without the benefits. We turned off the trial on non-Windows platforms.\nL1039: But also in parallel, we investigated the underlying problem (not related to the R/W lock) more and found several improvements to how that lock was used that ended up solving the priority inversion problems even with the regular lock. Once those improvements landed, we no longer saw the R/W lock moving anything for Windows (unlike earlier results). So given that, I've turned off the experiment and reverted the code to the normal base::Lock.\nL1040: \nL1041: \nL1042: cite12†Image: Peter Kasting's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L1043: ### Peter Kasting\nL1044: \nL1045: unread,\nL1046: \nL1047: Nov 20, 2023, 3:08:29 PM 11/20/23\nL1048: \nL1049: \nL1050: \nL1051: \nL1052: \nL1053: \nL1054: \nL1055: to Alexei Svitkine, Gabriel Charette, Robert Liao, Luc Nguyen, Łukasz Anforowicz, Etienne Bergeron, K. Moon, Peter Boström, cxx\nL1056: \nL1057: There is some fascinating work in this bug.\nL1058: \nL1059: I would love to hear a brews and bites talk about the odyssey some time.\nL1060: \nL1061: PK\nL1062: \nL1063: \nL1064: \nL1065: cite15†Image: Gabriel Charette's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com L1066: ### Gabriel Charette\nL1067: \nL1068: unread,\nL1069: \nL1070: Nov 22, 2023, 3:17:46 PM 11/22/23\nL1071: \nL1072: \nL1073: \nL1074: \nL1075: \nL1076: \nL1077: \nL1078: to Peter Kasting, Alexei Svitkine, Gabriel Charette, Robert Liao, Luc Nguyen, Łukasz Anforowicz, Etienne Bergeron, K. Moon, Peter Boström, cxx\nL1079: \nL1080: On Mon, Nov 20, 2023 at 5:08 PM Peter Kasting <pkas...@google.com> wrote:\nL1081: \nL1082: > There is some fascinating work in this bug.\nL1083: >\nL1084: > I would love to hear a brews and bites talk about the odyssey some time.\nL1085: \nL1086: +1 :)!\nL1087: \nL1088: \nL1089: \nL1090: Reply all\nL1091: \nL1092: Reply to author\nL1093: \nL1094: Forward\nL1095: \nL1096: \nL1097: \nL1098: 0 new messages\nL1099: \nL1100: \nL1101: \nL1102: Search\n"
